    "Stem": "On January 1, 2026, NorthStar Equipment issued $1,000,000 face value, 5-year bonds paying 8% annual interest when the market rate was 10%. The controller's amortization schedule shows an initial carrying value of $924,184.\n\nUsing the effective interest method, what is the interest expense for the first year?",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The correct answer is $92,418. Under the effective interest method, interest expense = carrying value of the bond × market rate at issuance. Carrying value on January 1 = $924,184. Market rate = 10%. Interest expense = $924,184 × 10% = $92,418. The difference between interest expense ($92,418) and cash interest paid ($80,000 = $1,000,000 × 8%) is $12,418, which amortizes the bond discount and increases the carrying value. A common CMA exam trap is using the face value or stated rate instead of the carrying value and market rate.",
    "QuestionID": "P1E-A-043",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part1OnlyFlag": true,
    "ReviewNote": "If missed or marked, review the linked study materials and rework the underlying concept without looking at the answer.",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "$100,000 is the result of multiplying face value ($1,000,000) by the market rate (10%). While the market rate is correct, the effective interest method uses the carrying value ($924,184), not the face value. This is a common error when candidates confuse the face value with the carrying value of bonds issued at a discount.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "$80,000 is the cash interest payment (face value × stated rate). This is not the interest expense under the effective interest method — it is the contractual cash outflow. The difference between interest expense and cash interest represents amortization of the bond discount or premium. A candidate selecting this may be using the cash-basis approach rather than the accrual-based effective interest method.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "$73,935 is the result of multiplying carrying value ($924,184) by the stated rate (8%). The stated rate determines the cash interest payment, but the effective interest method requires the market rate at issuance to compute interest expense. This error reverses the correct formula: carrying value × market rate.",
