    "Stem": "Profit margin ratio =:",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The profit margin ratio is a key profitability measure that shows how much of each sales dollar becomes profit. The formula is Profit Margin = Net Income divided by Sales Revenue. For example, if a company reports net income of $50,000 on sales of $500,000, its profit margin is 10%. This ratio is tested under CMA Part 1 Section C.2 (Performance Management) as a component of the DuPont analysis framework. The profit margin is distinct from the operating margin, which uses operating income instead of net income in the numerator. A common exam trap is to confuse profit margin with asset turnover (Sales / Assets) or return on assets (Net Income / Assets), which are separate components of the DuPont decomposition. When profit margin declines, management should investigate whether revenues are falling, costs are rising, or both — making this ratio a diagnostic tool, not just a formula to memorize.",
