    "Stem": "Cost pool in ABC is:",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "In Activity-Based Costing (ABC), a cost pool is a grouping of individual overhead costs that are driven by the same activity or cost driver. Rather than accumulating all overhead into a single plant-wide pool, ABC creates separate activity cost pools such as machine setup costs, material handling costs, quality inspection costs, and engineering change costs. Each pool is then allocated to products using a cost driver that reflects the consumption of that specific activity (e.g., number of setups, number of material moves, inspection hours). This approach provides more accurate product costing than traditional volume-based methods because it recognizes that different products consume overhead activities in different proportions. This concept is tested under CMA Part 1 Section D.3 (Cost Management — Activity-Based Costing). A common exam trap is confusing ABC cost pools with traditional departmental overhead pools — ABC pools are defined by activities that cross departmental boundaries, not by organizational departments.",
