    "Stem": "Cycle time is a:",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Cycle time is a key performance indicator classified within the Balanced Scorecard's Internal Business Process perspective. It measures the total elapsed time from the start to the completion of a process, reflecting operational efficiency and throughput. In the BSC framework, the four perspectives are: Financial (e.g., ROI, profit margin, EVA), Customer (e.g., satisfaction scores, on-time delivery, market share), Internal Business Process (e.g., cycle time, first-pass yield, defect rates, process cost), and Learning and Growth (e.g., employee training hours, turnover rate, skill certification rates). Cycle time reduction is a common operational goal because shorter cycle times reduce working capital tied up in work-in-process inventory and improve responsiveness to customer demand. This concept is tested under CMA Part 1 Section C.3 (Performance Management — Balanced Scorecard), where candidates must correctly classify performance measures into the appropriate BSC perspective. A common exam trap is confusing internal process measures with customer measures — cycle time benefits customers indirectly but is an internal operational metric, not a direct customer-facing measure like on-time delivery percentage.",
