    "Stem": "A cost driver is:",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "A cost driver is any factor or activity that causes a change in the total cost of an activity or cost object. In Activity-Based Costing (ABC), cost drivers are used to assign activity costs to products or services based on consumption. Cost drivers are classified as either volume-based (e.g., machine hours, direct labor hours, units produced) or transaction-based (e.g., number of purchase orders, number of setups, number of inspections). Transaction-based drivers are more precise for ABC because they capture the complexity and diversity of production activities that traditional volume-based allocation misses. For example, if setup costs are driven by the number of production runs (not by production volume), the cost driver 'number of setups' better reflects the cause of setup costs. This concept is tested under CMA Part 1 Section D.2 (Cost Management — cost measurement concepts). A common exam trap is confusing the cost driver (the causal factor) with the cost pool (the accumulated costs), or with the output measure (the result of production).",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "certification_date": "2026-07-22",
    "certification_batch": "R14 Wave 7",
