    "Stem": "Under the COSO Internal Control — Integrated Framework (2013), internal control is designed to provide reasonable assurance regarding the achievement of objectives in which of the following categories?",
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The COSO 2013 framework defines internal control as a process designed to provide reasonable assurance regarding the achievement of objectives relating to operations, reporting, and compliance. Operations objectives pertain to the effectiveness and efficiency of the entity's operations, including operational and financial performance goals and safeguarding of assets. Reporting objectives relate to internal and external financial and non-financial reporting, encompassing reliability, timeliness, transparency, and other terms as established by regulators, standard-setters, or the entity's policies. Compliance objectives pertain to adherence to laws and regulations to which the entity is subject. These three categories are distinct but may overlap — for example, a control designed to ensure accurate financial reporting (reporting objective) may also support compliance with securities regulations (compliance objective). Strategic objectives are not one of the three COSO categories; they are addressed through the entity's strategic planning and governance processes, not through the internal control framework directly.",
