    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Internal controls are classified as either preventive or detective based on when they act in relation to an error or irregularity. Preventive controls are designed to deter errors or fraud before they occur — they proactively stop undesirable events. Detective controls are designed to identify errors or fraud after they have occurred — they discover undesirable events that have already happened. Security cameras serve a dual purpose: the visible presence of cameras deters theft (preventive), but recorded footage can also identify a thief after the fact (detective). Badge access to the warehouse is a preventive control — it physically prevents unauthorized individuals from entering the premises. The annual physical inventory count is a detective control — it identifies discrepancies between recorded inventory and actual physical inventory after any errors or theft have occurred. The key exam distinction: preventive controls act like a lock on a door (stopping entry), while detective controls act like an alarm (alerting after entry has occurred).",
