    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Riverside Supply is a small business with only four accounting staff members. Due to limited personnel, the company cannot fully segregate cash handling, recording, and reconciliation duties. The owner currently reviews all bank reconciliations, approves all disbursements over $1,000, and compares actual-to-budget variances monthly. However, the external auditor has recommended enhancing controls. The owner has a limited budget of $5,000 and is evaluating which additional control measure would provide the greatest incremental risk reduction. Which recommendation should the owner implement?",
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The most effective incremental recommendation is to implement a mandatory vacation and job rotation policy, paired with cross-training a second employee to perform bank reconciliations during the primary employee's absence. Rationale: Compensating controls — such as the owner's existing review and approval activities — mitigate the segregation-of-duties gap, but they depend entirely on the owner's diligence. Adding mandatory vacation and job rotation introduces an independent check: when an employee is absent and another performs their duties, irregularities are more likely to surface. This is consistent with COSO Principle 10 (Control Activities — selects and develops control activities) and COSO Principle 12 (deploys through policies and procedures). The cross-training component ensures continuity. Budget constraint analysis: at $5,000 or less for training and policy implementation, this option is cost-feasible. Trade-off assessment: compared to hiring a fifth employee ($50,000+/year), an ERP implementation ($20,000+), or an external hotline service ($8,000+/year), mandatory vacation/job rotation provides the highest risk-reduction per dollar because it leverages existing personnel to create a control that specifically targets the identified gap (lack of independent review). The owner's existing compensating controls provide the foundation; mandatory rotation adds the detection layer that was missing. While no single $5,000 measure can achieve full segregation of duties, this option addresses the most critical residual risk — undetected irregularities in a single-person role — at a feasible cost.",
