    "ExplanationWrongA": "Hiring a fifth employee ($50,000+/year) exceeds the $5,000 budget by a factor of 10, making this recommendation infeasible under the stated constraint. Even if budget were unlimited, Riverside has only four accounting staff — adding one person does not automatically achieve segregation of duties unless at least four distinct incompatible functions (authorization, custody, recording, reconciliation) can be split across separate individuals. With five employees and four functions, segregation is possible but thin; a single absence or collusion risk remains. The recommendation is directionally correct (more staff supports segregation) but fails the budget feasibility test and overstates the effectiveness of a single incremental hire for a company this small.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "An ERP system with automated segregation-of-duties enforcement ($20,000+ for small-business implementation) also exceeds the $5,000 budget by a wide margin. More importantly, ERP-based segregation-of-duties enforcement requires distinct user roles across functions — Riverside still has only four staff members. The system can prevent one person from performing two incompatible functions in the software, but it cannot create people to fill the separated roles. A candidate selecting this option correctly identifies that technology can enforce segregation but overlooks two facts: the budget constraint makes it infeasible, and automated enforcement alone does not solve the underlying staffing limitation. If one employee holds all four user credentials (as might happen in a four-person shop), the ERP's segregation rules provide no real protection.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "An external whistleblower hotline ($8,000+/year) exceeds the $5,000 budget and addresses a different risk category. Hotlines are detective controls under COSO Principle 14 (communicates externally) — they detect irregularities after they occur rather than preventing them or providing ongoing independent verification. While a hotline is a valuable component of a broader control environment (particularly for fraud deterrence), it does not directly address the segregation-of-duties gap identified in the scenario. A candidate selecting this option may be applying a generic 'best practice' recommendation without evaluating whether it targets the specific control weakness. The question asks which measure provides the greatest incremental risk reduction for this business — not which is a good practice in general. Cost-effectiveness analysis under a budget constraint requires matching the control to the risk.",
