    "ProductionStatus": "Production",
    "Stem": "Northern Plains Manufacturing has implemented the following controls: (1) all purchase orders over $10,000 require dual signatures, (2) the accounts payable system automatically matches invoices to purchase orders and receiving reports before payment, and (3) the IT system locks user accounts after three failed login attempts. Which of the following correctly classifies these controls?",
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Preventive controls are designed to deter errors or fraud before they occur by making undesirable events difficult or impossible. All three controls described in the scenario are preventive: dual signatures on large purchase orders prevent unauthorized commitments by requiring a second approval before the order is placed; three-way matching (invoice to PO to receiving report) prevents payment for goods not ordered or not received by blocking the payment before it is processed; account lockout after failed login attempts prevents unauthorized system access by stopping brute-force attacks before they succeed. These differ from detective controls, which identify errors after they occur (e.g., bank reconciliations, exception reports), and corrective controls, which fix problems after detection (e.g., backup restoration, error correction procedures). The CMA exam frequently tests the ability to classify controls by their timing relative to the error event. A useful framework: preventive = before the error, detective = after the error but before correction, corrective = fixing the error's consequences.",
