    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "ProductionStatus": "Production",
    "Stem": "Lakestone Corporation's order entry system performs the following: it rejects any sales order where the customer number does not exist in the master file, calculates the order total as quantity × unit price, and generates a daily report of all orders exceeding the customer's credit limit. When the system transmits orders to the warehouse for fulfillment, it requires a confirmation code before releasing the pick list. Under the input-processing-output classification of application controls, these four activities are, respectively:",
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Application controls are categorized by where they operate in the transaction processing cycle — input, processing, or output. Input controls ensure that data entering the system is authorized, accurate, and complete. Customer number validation (rejecting orders with invalid customer IDs) is an input control because it validates data at the point of entry before the transaction proceeds. Processing controls ensure that data is processed accurately during system operations. The calculation of order totals (quantity × unit price) is a processing control because it verifies the accuracy of computations performed by the system. Output controls ensure that system outputs are accurate, complete, and distributed to authorized recipients. The daily credit-limit-exceeded report is an output control because it produces a report of transactions for management review after processing. The confirmation code requirement before pick-list release is an output control at the boundary between the order entry system and the warehouse system — it ensures that data transmitted to another system is authorized and complete. The correct sequence is: validation of customer number (input), order total calculation (processing), daily credit report (output), and transmission confirmation (output).",
