    "Stem": "In the context of IT controls, which of the following correctly distinguishes a disaster recovery plan (DRP) from a business continuity plan (BCP)?",
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The disaster recovery plan (DRP) and the business continuity plan (BCP) are related but distinct components of organizational resilience. The DRP focuses specifically on the restoration of IT infrastructure, systems, applications, and data after a disruptive event. It addresses technical recovery procedures such as restoring servers from backups, failing over to alternate data centers, recovering databases, and reestablishing network connectivity. The DRP defines recovery time objectives (RTO — how quickly systems must be restored) and recovery point objectives (RPO — how much data loss is acceptable). The BCP is broader in scope — it addresses the continuity of all critical business operations, including IT-dependent processes but also manual workarounds, alternate facilities, communication with stakeholders, and the decision-making framework for declaring a disaster and activating recovery procedures. The BCP encompasses the DRP as a subset; the DRP is the IT-focused component within the broader business continuity framework. A common exam trap is to reverse this relationship — treating the BCP as a subset of the DRP — which is incorrect because business continuity includes non-IT functions (personnel, facilities, supply chain) that disaster recovery does not address.",
