    "ProductionStatus": "Production",
    "Stem": "Omega Financial Services is conducting its annual risk assessment. The risk management team wants to identify emerging risks related to a new cryptocurrency custody product the company plans to launch. Because no team member has significant cryptocurrency experience, the team decides to survey external experts anonymously over multiple rounds, with each round's aggregated results shared before the next round. This technique is best described as:",
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The technique described is the Delphi method, a structured group communication process used for risk identification when expert input is needed but subject-matter experts are geographically dispersed, anonymous input is desired to prevent dominance by strong personalities, or the topic involves significant uncertainty. Key characteristics of the Delphi method include: (1) anonymous participation — experts respond independently without knowing who else is participating; (2) multiple iterative rounds — after each round, aggregated (not individual) responses are shared, and participants can revise their responses based on the group's collective input; (3) facilitation by a coordinator who designs the questionnaires and compiles responses between rounds; and (4) convergence toward consensus over successive rounds. The Delphi method is particularly useful for emerging-technology risk assessment because it surfaces perspectives that might be suppressed in face-to-face meetings where junior participants defer to senior colleagues. The CMA exam tests the ability to distinguish Delphi from other risk identification techniques such as brainstorming (unstructured, interactive, single session), SWOT analysis (internal/external framework), and scenario analysis (narrative-based exploration of possible futures).",
