    "ExplanationCorrect": "The $2.5 million represents inherent risk — the risk to the entity in the absence of any management actions to alter either the risk's likelihood or its impact. Inherent risk is the raw, untreated exposure before controls are applied. The $400,000 is the residual risk — the risk that remains after management implements risk responses (in this case, hedging and dual-approval controls). The risk assessment process follows a logical flow: (1) identify risks → (2) assess inherent risk (magnitude without controls) → (3) evaluate existing or planned controls → (4) determine residual risk (remaining exposure after controls) → (5) decide whether residual risk is within the organization's risk appetite or requires additional response. The distinction between inherent and residual risk is fundamental to the COSO risk assessment component and is tested on the CMA exam because it connects risk identification to control design decisions.",
