    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under COSO Principle 12 (Control Activities), detective controls are designed to identify errors or irregularities after they have occurred — they detect, rather than prevent. A reconciliation of processed claims to the claims register compares two independently maintained data sources to identify discrepancies, making it a classic detective control. The key distinction: preventive controls stop problems before they occur (e.g., approval requirements, segregation of duties, access restrictions), while detective controls find problems after the fact (e.g., reconciliations, exception reports, audits, physical inventory counts, analytical reviews). The CMA exam frequently tests this classification by presenting operational examples and asking the candidate to correctly categorize them. Note that COSO does not use 'detective' as a formal category label but describes controls by timing — before (preventive), during (concurrent), or after (detective) the transaction. In practice, many frameworks, including the CMA exam, use the term 'detective controls' following common professional usage from auditing standards.",
