    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under COSO Principle 16 (Monitoring Activities), ongoing evaluations are built into the entity's recurring operating activities — they are continuous, real-time, and performed by the people responsible for the process as part of their regular duties. Examples include: supervisory reviews of daily transaction reports, exception reporting from automated systems, and regular management review of key performance indicators. Separate evaluations, by contrast, are conducted periodically by objective parties not directly involved in the process — such as internal audit engagements, quality assurance reviews, and external assessments. The key distinction tested on the CMA exam is temporal integration: ongoing evaluations are continuous and embedded in operations (they happen during the process), while separate evaluations occur after the fact at discrete intervals. Both forms are necessary — ongoing evaluations provide timely, continuous feedback, while separate evaluations offer independent, objective assessment. The mix and frequency depend on the nature of risks and the rate of change in the business environment.",
