    "Stem": "COSO Principle 13 requires that the organization obtain or generate and use relevant, quality information to support the functioning of internal control. The quality characteristic that addresses whether information is available to decision-makers before it loses its capacity to influence decisions is:",
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under COSO Principle 13 (Information and Communication), information must possess certain quality characteristics to support effective internal control. These characteristics include: (1) accessibility — information must be readily available to those who need it; (2) correctness — information must be accurate and reliable; (3) currency — information must be sufficiently up-to-date; (4) protection — information must be safeguarded against unauthorized access; (5) retention — information must be preserved for an appropriate period; and (6) sufficiency — enough information of the right level of detail must be available. Timeliness (or currency) addresses the temporal dimension: information that arrives after a decision has been made has no value for that decision. The CMA exam tests these dimensions because poor-quality information undermines every other component of internal control — risk assessments based on stale data, control activities that operate on incorrect inputs, and monitoring that uses outdated metrics all fail regardless of how well those components are designed.",
